    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 150539\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1030 PM MST Mon Sep 14 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Abundant moisture will remain situated across the eastern half of \nArizona providing enhanced chances for showers and thunderstorms \nthrough the middle of the week, especially over the mountain \nlocations.\n\n- There will be the potential for some storms to produce strong \nwinds, but the greater threat will be heavy rainfall and localized \nflash flooding.\n\n- Shower and thunderstorm chances will diminish by the end of the \nweek with the introduction of drier air. \n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures are expected throughout \n  the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Wednesday/...\nLatest objective analysis indicates the subtropical ridge of high \npressure centered over the southeastern states while broad upper \nlevel troughing remains situated over the northwestern states. This \noverall pattern configuration continues to support more of a south \nto southwest flow across much AZ while more westerly flow, bringing \ndrier air, is occurring from the Lower Colorado River Valley \nwestward. The south to southwest flow entrenched across much of AZ \nis expected to remain in place through the middle of the week, \naiding with periodic moisture influxes as PWAT values peak above \n1.5\" along with low-level mixing ratios of 12-14 g/kg. Therefore, an \nelevated potential for shower and thunderstorm activity will remain \nin place, especially from Phoenix eastward through the middle of the \nweek before chances diminish towards the end of the week once the \ndrier air filters in.\n\nThe combination of plentiful moisture and strong daytime heating \nshould result in good instability once again as MUCAPE values are \nexpected to peak at 1000-1500 J/KG. The latest HREF membership shows \nsoutheast AZ becoming convectively active during the afternoon hours \nwith some of this activity drifting into the higher terrain areas to \nthe east and southeast of Phoenix with more isolated activity across \nthe south-central AZ lower deserts. Similar to the last couple of \ndays, despite the very favorable thermodynamic parameters in place, \nthe lack of good upper-level forcing for ascent continues to be a \nlimiting factor for more widespread activity. With these storms, \nheavy rainfall leading to localized flash flooding will be the \nprimary threat with strong downburst wind gusts a secondary threat \ngiven that DCAPE values will still be sufficiently high, ranging \nbetween 1000-1200 J/KG. Quieter conditions can be expected this \nevening into at least the first part of the overnight period, before \npotentially another round of elevated showers and storms develops, \nmost likely after 3 AM MST. The latest hi-res guidance is indicating \nthe best potential over the foothills and higher terrain to north \nand east of Phoenix. However, the convective threat late in the \novernight hours into early Tuesday morning will be highly \nconditional of whether or not a gravity wave/outflow will be a \nsufficient triggering mechanism for development as the overall \nlarger scale forcing for ascent will still be lacking.\n\nBetter upper-level support is more likely to materialize, especially \nheading into late Tuesday through Wednesday, as a weak mid-level low \ncurrently located over the southern tip of the Baja Peninsula in \nconjunction with another weak upper-level low off the west coast of \nthe Baja Peninsula move into the region. Therefore, these features \nare likely to pair up and be sufficient to trigger more widespread \nshower and thunderstorm activity across much of central and eastern \nAZ. Latest NBM is honing in on this increasing coverage as it shows \nPoPs increasing to 50-80% from Phoenix eastward from Tuesday evening \nthrough Wednesday. The main concern will be an increasing threat for \nflash flooding given the abundant moisture in place with both the \nECMWF and GFS indicating PWATs exceeding 1.8\" along with low-level \nmixing ratios as high as 14 g/kg. In addition, MLCAPE values are \nlikely to range between 1000-1500 J/KG providing plenty of \ninstability. Therefore, the environment is likely to become \nfavorable for these thunderstorms to produce high rainfall rates in \nexcess of 1-2\"/hr, leading to that enhanced flash flood potential. A \nFlood Watch will likely be needed for portions of the area from late \nTuesday through Wednesday.\n\nTemperatures through the middle of the week will continue to run \nslightly above normal across the western deserts as high top out \naround 105 degrees, while more clouds and potential rainfall will \nkeep highs closer to 100 degrees across the south-central AZ lower \ndeserts. In fact, with the greater likelihood of more abundant cloud \ncover as well as higher rainfall potential, afternoon highs on \nWednesday across much of the south-central AZ lower deserts are now \nforecast to top out in the 90s. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Thursday through Sunday/...\nGuidance is starting to agree on a quicker drying trend later this \nweek as the Pacific trough is favored to move farther into the Great \nBasin and at least into northern and western portions of the Desert \nSouthwest. This is likely to push drier air eastward through most if \nnot all of Arizona as early as Thursday but more so Friday into \nSaturday. Thursday is still a bit of an uncertain day as rain \nchances may still linger across the south-central lower deserts and \ndefinitely over eastern Arizona. However, by Friday, enough dry air \nmoving in should end chances over the lower deserts and really limit \nchances over the high terrain. It is doubtful we will completely \nscour out all of the moisture across the higher terrain, so 10-20% \nPoPs remain each day across eastern Gila County through the weekend. \n\nAs conditions dry out into the weekend, temperatures are likely to \nface a bit of a warming trend but as of now the region is favored to \nstay under broad upper level troughing into the weekend. Lower \ndesert highs are expected to warm to above normal by Friday or \nSaturday, but so far the NBM indicates a most likely range of 103-\n107 degrees. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0530Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe potential for SHRA/TSRA and associated impacts will be the \nprimary weather concern through Tuesday night under frequent cloud \ndecks 7K ft and above. Confidence is good that E/SE winds will \nprevail through the morning with the majority of SHRA/TSRA lifting \ninto higher terrain north and east of terminals. However, a few \nSHRA/TSRA could redevelop near terminals prior to sunrise, and have \nmaintained a Prob30 mention. \n\nForecast confidence deteriorates Tuesday afternoon and evening \ncontingent on activity in the morning. Moderate confidence exists \nthat winds remain E/SE or become southerly during the afternoon with \nany additional TS holding off until the evening. Development and \ncoverage of storms Tuesday evening remains highly uncertain, however \nsomewhat better confidence exists regarding more widespread \nSHRA/TSRA and significant operational impacts late Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday morning. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo weather concerns will exist through Tuesday night under mostly \nclear skies. Winds will continue to behave similarly to the past 24 \nhours varying between southeast and west at KIPL, and southerly \nat KBLH with local afternoon gusts 20-25 kt. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nPeriods of scattered showers and thunderstorms are likely through \nWednesday across the eastern districts supporting a localized heavy \nrainfall threat. Moisture levels will remain elevated except for \nsoutheast California. Expect minimum humidity levels to still fall \ninto a 15-25% range for the western districts while the eastern \ndistricts will only dip to 30-45% through Wednesday. Winds will \ngenerally follow diurnal patterns across the eastern districts, \nwhile much of the western districts will have elevated southerly \nwinds with occasional gusts above 20 mph. By Thursday into Friday, \nmuch drier air is forecast to spread eastward across the region, \neventually ending rain chances for all but the far eastern Arizona \nhigh terrain and lowering humidities into the teens across the lower \ndeserts.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
